  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 PM CDT Thu May 21 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper troughing aloft generally influences our region's weather\nthrough Saturday. Shortwave trough within this flow will bring \nwidespread rain Friday and Saturday. Prior to this occurring, \npresently dry air mass over the Upper Midwest combine with \ngusty winds and lingering dry fuels contributing to near \ncritical fire weather conditions this afternoon in northern Red\nRiver Valley into northwest Minnesota.\n\nInto next week, flow becomes more zonal to southwesterly amid\nupper ridging into the Northern Plains by mid to late next week.\nThis will increase overall temperature trend, allowing more\nsummer-like temperatures into the 80s across our area by\nTuesday, perhaps as early as Monday. This increases the chance\nfor instability and some moisture to work its way into our\nregion, however flow aloft is favored to be on the lighter side,\nlimiting the potential for organization of thunderstorms \n(should they occur), as well as limited chance for more robust\nforcing aloft to spark convection. There is still a chance for \ndisorganized scattered showers and storms as ignited by other \nsources of forcing like surface/topographically induced \nforcing mid to late next week, with confidence in details like\nlocation and timing very low.\n\n...Near critical fire weather conditions...\n\nGusty south winds around 25 mph linger in portions of the \nnorthern Red River Valley, and will continue until just before\nsunset. This combined with mid 20s RH currently in the Red River\nValley (as well as into northwest Minnesota), is driving near\ncritical fire weather conditions. These conditions end around or\njust before sunset.\n\n...Friday into Saturday rainfall...\n\nCompact but vigorous shortwave trough will bring a swath of\nwidespread rain into the area from south to north starting\nFriday afternoon, lasting into Saturday morning. Guidance has \ntrended in an increase in amounts, likely owing to a trend\ntoward a stronger passage of this system as it swings into and\nthrough the eastern Dakotas and western Minnesota in a\nnegatively tilted fashion.\n\nWhat once appeared like high end amounts around half an inch is\nnow more likely to be widespread half an inch of rainfall, with\npotential to see around 1 inch. At this time, best chance for 1\ninch is within southeast North Dakota where rainfall may linger\nthe longest.\n\nThunder chances still look very low with most guidance keeping\nCAPE values under 300 J/kg. Can't rule out a few embedded\nlightning strikes given the strength of forcing, but overall\nactivity is expected to be rather low.",
